# Comment convention

A comment states a constraint, invariant, or consequence that the code cannot express. Nothing else.

If a reader could learn the same thing by reading the line below it, delete the comment.

## Write comments for

- **Constraints imposed from outside the code**: a protocol requirement, an OS behaviour, a WoW client
  quirk, a Docker or SSH limitation. Say what breaks if the constraint is violated.
- **Invariants a future edit could quietly break**: why an order matters, why a value is capped, why a
  failure is swallowed, why a set is exactly this set.
- **Consequences that are not local**: this write reaches every player's install; this path runs
  elevated; this list is mirrored in another project.
- **Non-obvious choices between two reasonable options**: why the slower call, why the shell-out
  instead of the library.

## Never write

- **History.** No `used to`, `no longer`, `previously`, `before this`, `that broke`, `the exact
  scenario`, `the bug this fixes`. History belongs in the commit message and the pull request, where it
  stays attached to the change instead of following the code around forever.

  The test is tense, not vocabulary. "The details payload no longer returns the root password" narrates
  a change and rots; "MapAsync withholds the root password" states the same constraint and stays true.
  A present-tense counterfactual is fine — "`up -d` on a running container would otherwise be a no-op"
  describes what this code prevents, not what someone once fixed.
- **Notes to the reviewer.** No `NOTE:`, `IMPORTANT:`, `Deliberately`, `On purpose`, `Careful!`. If it
  matters, state the constraint plainly; if it does not, drop it.
- **Restatements of the next line.** `// Increment the counter`, `// Import the module`,
  `// Return the result`.
- **Commented-out code.** Delete it. Git remembers.
- **Section banners and decoration** beyond what the file already uses consistently.

## Doc comments

XML `<summary>` on a public or internal API stays. It says what the member is for and what a caller has
to know, in at most three lines. Longer explanations go in the body of the type's summary, not on every
member.

TSDoc on exported frontend functions and hooks follows the same rule.

## Applying this to existing code

This is a judgement pass, file by file. Phrase matching produces false positives — plenty of legitimate
API docs contain "used to" or "instead of" — so read the comment and the code before deleting.

`wdbx/WDBXEditor/` is vendored third-party code. Leave its comments alone.
